71 "The parabola $y = x^2 + 2$ and the line $y = 3x$ intersect at the points $(x_1, y_1)$ and $(x_2, y_2),$ where $x_1 <        x_2.$ What is the value of $x_1 + x_2?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216959 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "44ed3aff-8a4b-520c-808f-9e3ff928ac74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216959 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+2 \\\\ y=3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2 & =3x \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x_1 = 1$ and $x_2 = 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nx_1 + x_2 = 1 + 2 = 3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216960 #edb/ref 1069 "e-19815" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216960 #edb/ref 1071 "The parabola ![](images/49/49d58a04c172fb017d3e63107af8686694a70f8b3b281b61f1bf80fbbce1903c.svg) and the line ![](images/da/dad6e7d0144b11544800cab8e15352011d03b14a429fd6b38518e6d764777fbb.svg) intersect at the points ![](images/58/586d275bc1ac46d094c469d88dfd59d0a63d2ede5496929e15923c42b180fd47.svg) and ![](images/af/afa2f1d8401bc59845387bc9c7d1d6b765abe9c638b3c28f1914f6bd9458e348.svg) where ![](images/ba/bac1330d96cc473aab0c7d4fb929105e307a215a8e6031084e7c9cc61cb87b66.svg) What is the value of ![](images/f5/f52303e047d6e35cf5061a15e4136e4f253bf4cdd2d421145f8a58b05389a469.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216960 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9aa14c81-aeaa-51b7-9d16-468e22bc113d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216960 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n![](images/31/318d27072067aa70d893f2a1c0dc118ad7caced10b9ca5567b5bb0f91638ef30.svg)\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n![](images/c3/c3bb314de0a4b80fb6d51bbb155484aa968fee78d81011fd578cc8b8bca04df2.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/25/25273d659db203df5af353cdda1a390bdbb635b59363390a0ca00b45fdd24bb2.svg) the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-coordinates of the intersection points are ![](images/4f/4fe2b5629a57d8e984a53a0887f0ab10595a82fe303f8c70080cff9039ee200f.svg) and ![](images/42/428ebea63626be22a9e5b18f6f4bd8ceba019bd6d3e685a0621e843604f0f826.svg) \n\nTo find the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either the curve or the line. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ![](images/e3/e3f9686f4fa57342e9e4415fe494e0361ef79005a9c92c2192d11f8da980481f.svg) we get\n![](images/6b/6bf49ff9ecea5536117e06be2a55625e2dd056609abec901f615ad74bd52c290.svg)\nso one point of intersection is ![](images/2f/2f7731330530547c0207184c710622d49d8b696419aaba6572ae0b65cc3672ee.svg)\n\n- Substituting ![](images/d7/d7657c785332cdb454794e479e9aae39f6d6f92532f10912f85c1bf3bc2fb317.svg) we get\n![](images/40/406ee9901d6670c3ddb3774fc575c5a1a80d33668dcb345a7a494f297df64147.svg)\nso the other point of intersection is ![](images/f0/f07ed4790b4cd3a20a4af1c48d680f6e6681feb972d35075171975f967eea468.svg)\n\nTherefore, ![](images/1d/1d7e290e5d4bac0018851250cc98d151231495c6cc9187aedaec060d1f9579bf.svg) and\n![](images/a5/a5c25be7330db098773c5566f2ce52d8f6cb65f06e0e9fc9bc4eb0b92a0ba534.svg)\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/da/da3cbc81f8170e3cf098a97d249e3ad8f0abf358fa641d8b94b1eb301f1a7858.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216961 #edb/ref 1069 "e-19816" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216961 #edb/ref 1071 "The parabola $y=-x^2+6x$ and the line $y=9$ intersect at a single point with coordinates $(p,q).$ What is $p+q?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216961 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b24d4adc-8623-5fa9-9ac3-f95d7b4c0d1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216961 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-{x}^{2}+6x \\\\ y=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+6x & =9 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+6x-9 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-6x+9) & =0 \\\\ -(x-3)(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=3.$ \n\nWe know the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point from the equation of the line. So, the only point of intersection is $(3, 9).$ Therefore, $p=3,\\; q=9,$ and\n\n$$\np+q = 3+9 = 12.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/f9/f9d09e07988752af26143fa2dc8df260784be5fcb16c28d8115bb44fc3c1a4a1.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216962 #edb/ref 1069 "e-19817" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216962 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the line $y=x +2k$ does **not** intersect the parabola $y=x^2-x+3,$ what is the range of possible values of $k?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216962 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b2ead57b-8fd7-5d98-93fe-0e06290a5aa3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186216962 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine whether the line $y=x +2k$ intersects the parabola $y=x^2-x+3,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}-x+3 \\\\ y=x+2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x+3 & =x+2k \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+3-2k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this equation using the quadratic formula with $a=1,\\, b=-2,$ and $c=3-2k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(3-2k)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{4-12+8k}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{8k-8}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line and parabola do not intersect, our quadratic equation must have no real solutions; hence, the expression inside the square root must be negative.\nSetting up and solving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8k-8 & <0 \\\\ 8k & <8 \\\\ k & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k \\lt   1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217108 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217108 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217108 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217108 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186217329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217108 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186217334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217108 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217108 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217108 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the X-Coordinates of the Intersection Points of a Parabola and a Line" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217108 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186216959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217108 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "31b2bec9-3dff-557c-8cf5-a54f145fc45c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217109 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186217330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217109 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186217335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217109 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217109 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217109 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Y-Coordinates of the Intersection Points of a Parabola and a Line" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217109 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186216960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217109 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "797cb8e2-0230-56ec-8fda-7f2b40a2329c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217110 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186217331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217110 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186217339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217110 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217110 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217110 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Intersection Point of a Parabola and a Line" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217110 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186216961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217110 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "8cf321a6-6ef6-58a2-ab52-176a923fc086" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217111 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217111 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217111 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186217332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217111 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186217336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217111 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217111 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217111 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Range of an Unknown Parameter Given That There Are No Intersection Points" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217111 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186216962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217111 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "76453c35-6f8e-5564-80f7-33fc0c8edbde" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217314 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186217980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217314 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186217981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217314 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186217982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217314 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186217983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217314 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186217984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217314 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186217985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217314 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217314 #edb/ref 1045 "Finding Intersections of Lines and Quadratic Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217314 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186217980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217314 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217314 #edb/ref 1050 1080.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217314 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f67186a8-3fe3-5bdd-b09e-4edfe15e8cef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217980 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186217981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217980 #edb/ref 1061 509403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217980 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a89077dd-e912-5270-b496-6fe6281d817b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217980 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186218320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217981 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186217982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217981 #edb/ref 1061 509404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217981 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1e5979d0-7029-563e-b231-e335fadaa16d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217981 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186217108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217982 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186217983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217982 #edb/ref 1061 509408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217982 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "67ccd293-c59d-59e8-a1d5-686a88bbfc0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217982 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186217109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217983 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186217984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217983 #edb/ref 1061 509412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217983 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5955a5e4-10f4-59b2-8eb7-8c63323e0526" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217983 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186218321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217984 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186217985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217984 #edb/ref 1061 509413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217984 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d1bb51f4-11dc-57e8-988f-57c86f8a0159" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217984 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186217110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217985 #edb/ref 1061 509417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217985 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3812edf3-d3fb-5828-8887-e0cd46b17f0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186217985 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186217111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218320 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the line $y = x + 2$ and the parabola $y = x^2 + x + 1,$ shown in the graph below.\n\n![](images/9d/9d7ca10163c9b5096f90e19bdb50d98881bd55e92cbaea4aa5af6696e728f9f3.png)\n\nWe can see from the diagram that there are two points of intersection. So let's calculate the coordinates of these intersection points.\n\nAn intersection point is a common solution of the equations $y = x + 2$ and $y = x^2 + x + 1.$ In other words, to get the coordinates of the intersection points, we have to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+x+1 \\\\ y=x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and then solving for $x$ using our knowledge of quadratic equations, we get\n![](images/96/9618f0221da97c281424def31e5d2a8fb96422b800928ad4ec4a01ed4dbad3d7.svg)\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ \n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting $x=−1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\  & =(-1)+2 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(-1,1).$\n\n- Substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+2 \\\\  & =(1)+2 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(1,3).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the points of intersection are $\\left(-1,\\,1 \\right)$ and $\\left(1,\\,3 \\right).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218320 #edb/ref 1065 13862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218320 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b907a9fb-d730-5d33-b47d-e448e4bc64dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218320 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186218321 #edb/ref 1064 "![](images/b9/b9cb25bbb7b3076a3dae8688a232d18ec4092d68d9e815dedb438bc6900d97bb.png)\n\nA line and a parabola may have **two**, \n**one**, or **no** points of intersection.\n\nThis is similar to how a quadratic equation may have  **two**, \n**one**, or **no** solutions.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218621832