 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532605 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532605 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532605 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186532057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532605 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "eeeb0417-7377-5df8-aa31-302ace210d7c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532641 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532641 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532641 #edb/ref 1069 "q-250073" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532641 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532641 #edb/ref 1071 "The parabola $y={x}^{2}$ and the line $y=9x-14$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ Find the values of ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}.$\n\n${y}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{y}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532641 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3860fd8d-fa51-5495-8869-9f057f53d9a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532641 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2} \\\\ y=9x-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =9x-14 \\\\ {x}^{2}-9x+14 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-7) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2},$ the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=7.$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the parabola since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{1}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={x}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(2)^{2} \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,4).$\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{2}=7,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & ={x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(7)^{2} \\\\  & =49,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(7,49).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/4e/4e0ce399e32d7b957b71384d61cf08024b5d9995fde28d5f1fac71e1f123b2aa.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532663 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532663 #edb/ref 1069 "q-155534" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532663 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532663 #edb/ref 1071 "The parabola $y={x}^{2}+x$ and the line $y=3x-1$ intersect at a single point with coordinates $(p,q).$ What is $p+q?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532663 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "69684600-8c5e-5874-b78e-2cbd727505f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532663 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+x \\\\ y=3x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x & =3x-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-3x+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=1.$ \n\nTo find the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we plug this value into either curve. \nLet's choose the equation of the line. Substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x-1 \\\\  & =3(1)-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the only point of intersection is $(1,2).$ Therefore, $p=1,\\,q=2,$ and \n\n$$\np+q=1+2=3.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/53/53b8622b0d2fa3fa756d5520d24849fb633539fb471037b9fce6c97464a5fc20.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532672 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532672 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532672 #edb/ref 1069 "q-250076" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532672 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532672 #edb/ref 1071 "The parabola $y={x}^{2}+2x-3$ and the line $y=5x+7$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ Find the values of ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}.$\n\n${y}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{y}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532672 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "82d663f5-9675-5779-8562-0b4d4248c2b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532672 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+2x-3 \\\\ y=5x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x-3 & =5x+7 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x-10 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2},$ the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=-2$ and ${x}_{2}=5.$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{1}=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =5{x}_{1}+7 \\\\  & =5(-2)+7 \\\\  & =-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,-3).$\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =5{x}_{2}+7 \\\\  & =5(5)+7 \\\\  & =32,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(5,32).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/98/98d656438193d68da44573720da4a0fa5f7e1f7d253c29e0de2e309468c8b552.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532676 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532676 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532676 #edb/ref 1069 "q-250072" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532676 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532676 #edb/ref 1071 "The parabola $y=-{x}^{2}+3x+15$ and the line $y=4x-5$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ Find the values of ${x}_{1}$ and  ${x}_{2}.$\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532676 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8a3fea1f-f2c7-5a54-bfc4-1db59932f81e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532676 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-{x}^{2}+3x+15 \\\\ y=4x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+3x+15 & =4x-5 \\\\ -{x}^{2}-x+20 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}+x-20) & =0 \\\\ -(x+5)(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =-5,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=-5$ and ${x}_{2}=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532682 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532682 #edb/ref 1069 "q-152313" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532682 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532682 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the line $y=4x+k$ does **not** intersect the parabola $y={x}^{2}+6x+7,$ what is the range of possible values of $k?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532682 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9485be5f-7e9e-52c2-8dc7-d3a495a119a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532682 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine whether the line $y=4x+k$ intersects the parabola $y={x}^{2}+6x+7,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+6x+7 \\\\ y=4x+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6x+7 & =4x+k \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+7-k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this equation using the quadratic formula with $a=1,\\,b=2,$ and $c=7-k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{{2}^{2}-4(1)(7-k)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{4-28+4k}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{-24+4k}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line and parabola do not intersect, our quadratic equation must have no real solutions; hence, the expression inside the square root must be negative.\nSetting up and solving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-24+4k & <0 \\\\ 4k & <24 \\\\ k & <6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k<6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532696 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532696 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532696 #edb/ref 1069 "q-250085" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532696 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532696 #edb/ref 1071 "The parabola $y=-{x}^{2}-4x$ and the line $y=4$ intersect at the point $P.$ Find the coordinates of $P.$\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532696 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b39dbd28-21a4-5509-83b2-cf250304af27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532696 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-{x}^{2}-4x \\\\ y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-4x & =4 \\\\ -{x}^{2}-4x-4 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}+4x+4) & =0 \\\\ -(x+2)(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=-2.$\n\nWe know the y-coordinate of the intersection point from the equation of the line. So, the only point of intersection is $(-2,4).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/18/187c98be201ec084cdd4fb21f0347f9d768c251cfedcacc9532d754741199e14.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532707 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532707 #edb/ref 1069 "q-155523" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532707 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532707 #edb/ref 1071 "The parabola $y={x}^{2}-2$ and the line $y=x$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ What is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532707 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d109f13f-6c22-5d50-a9f4-da3fbec07962" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532707 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}-2 \\\\ y=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2 & =x \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-2 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-1+2=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532710 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532710 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532710 #edb/ref 1069 "q-250156" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532710 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532710 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the line $y=3x+2$ does **not** intersect the parabola $y={x}^{2}+5x+k,$ find the range of possible values of $k.$\n\n$k$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532710 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dace6513-0d3a-5c62-b857-525a505a8ea4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532710 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine whether the line $y=3x+2$ intersects the parabola $y={x}^{2}+5x+k,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+5x+k \\\\ y=3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x+k & =3x+2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+(k-2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this equation using the quadratic formula with $a=1,$ $b=2,$ and $c=k-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{(2)^{2}-4(1)(k-2)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{4-4k+8}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{12-4k}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line and parabola do not intersect, our quadratic equation must have no real solutions; hence, the expression inside the square root must be negative. Setting up and solving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-4k & <0 \\\\ -4k & <-12 \\\\ k & >3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k>3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
